<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Francis Xavier</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1506">1506</a>–<a href="/years/1552">1552</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Saint Francis Xavier</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Francis Xavier was a Navarrese priest and co-founder of the Society of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a> who became the leading Catholic missionary in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>. Based in Portuguese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goa">Goa</a> from 1542, he preached in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malacca">Malacca</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maluku-islands">Moluccas</a>, and in 1549 landed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kagoshima">Kagoshima</a>, opening the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> mission to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, where lords such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otomo-sorin">Otomo Sorin</a> received him. He died on an island off the coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> in 1552 and was canonised in 1622.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/francis-xavier.jpg" alt="Francis Xavier" width="320" height="408" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Franciscus_de_Xabier.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
